[00:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] &gt;&gt; Today, I\'m showing you how to use AI'

[00:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tools to create cinematic product commercials across different product categories. I\'m going to show you the whole process including the failures, the cost, and even how to save bad generations in post. Welcome back to the channel and if you\'re new here, hi. My'

[00:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'name is Thomas and I make videos about AI and product photography. So, if that\'s something you\'re into, consider subscribing. Last week, I posted a video where I tested out a new workflow to create cinematic product commercials with seed ants. And in this video, I'

[00:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wanted to test that workflow across three different product categories to see how low I could reduce the cost per commercial while still keeping the high quality output. And I definitely found a lot of things you should do and also'

[00:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'things you shouldn\'t do. So, thank you to Higgs Field for sponsoring this video and giving me the credits to play around with this workflow for you guys. So, if you want to follow along for yourself, all the prompts and assets and resources will be linked in the description below.'

[01:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But now, let\'s get into the commercials. &gt;&gt; Captain, the the rum it\'s gone. &gt;&gt; For the pirate commercial, I just'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'started with a simple reference image and a rough idea of how the commercial should play out. I knew I needed a captain, a pirate ship, and then the crewmates to present to the captain that the rum is gone. Then from there, I wanted the captain to look angry with'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'them and then somehow transition into the product being presented and the tagline at the end. To create that commercial, I opened Cinema Studio and created a new project. The very first thing I did is I created a product reference sheet for the bottle and save'

[02:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that as an element. Now, in the reference sheet, I have a full view of the bottle, but also a close-up of the label in order to give C Dance the best chances to get every single detail right on the label. I then used the cinematic'

[02:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'locations features to give me a bunch of alternatives for the captain\'s quarters and the pirate ship. And since it\'s super cheap generating using the cinematic locations feature, you can really dial in the look until you\'re satisfied. Then it was time to generate'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'our characters, so I started by generating the crewmates. And these were actually a simple one prompt result with the same prompt I got both characters. And now, if you\'re thinking that the prompt looks really complicated and you don\'t know how to do this yourself, I'

[02:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'would strongly recommend using Claude as your prompting assistant. You can literally copy the exact prompts that I use in this video and then just ask Claude to adjust those prompts for your characters and your scenes. So, always'

[02:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'when creating characters and concepts, I go back and forth with Claude to discuss the prompts that are going to be used, and I can just explain to Claude in plain language the changes I want, and it will make the changes for the'

[03:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prompts, which will then give a good result in Cinema Studio. Next, I wanted to create myself as the captain, so I uploaded a reference image of myself along with this prompt, and this is the result we got. And now, if you remember from the last video, an important step'

[03:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'when creating the character reference sheet is to remove the face from the full body view in order for C Dance not to confuse which face to use when generating the videos, and that way locking in that consistency much better.'

[03:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I also created this pen as a prop for the captain to use when the crewmates are coming to break the news for him. And now that we had created all the characters, locations, and props and saved those as elements, we could move'

[03:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on to the video creation part. And if you haven\'t downloaded the free shot list director skill that we\'re going to use in Claude, then you can do so from the description below. With the shot list director skill installed, I\'m going to upload a screenshot of the naming of'

[03:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the elements along with the assets so that Claude understands how to tag the assets in the prompts. So, basically what this does is that Claude understands how to tag the assets in the prompts and they will be automatically'

[04:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tagged when you copy and paste the prompt into Cinema Studio. A few important things to know before we start generating the videos is when you use the shot list director skill, I would always tell Claude how long you want the commercial to be because this way Claude'

[04:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'understands how to use the shot list director skill to build out the generation so it fits within your commercial length. Here, you can also try to tell Claude to reduce the number of generations in the shot list if you'

[04:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want to save credits. In my previous video, it took me 20 15-second long full HD quality C dance generations to get the final commercial done. So, for this one, I wanted to reduce of generations per commercial to a maximum of 10'

[04:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '15-second long C dance generations. And for this first one, I want to show you all the generations scene by scene so I can show you all the failures and also how to save the bad generations in post. For the opening scene of the shot list,'

[05:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we had four cuts and as you can see, two of the shots failed miserably. The opening shot is good with the ship going through the water, but then we had two shots which are complete failures where Jack Sparrow suddenly appeared and the'

[05:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pouring out of the empty bottle is looking super weird, but then the final fourth shot where the crewmates are delivering the line to the captain is spot on. Captain, the the rum, it\'s gone.'

[05:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Pay credits, we\'re not going to try to regenerate this whole shot, but we\'re going to take the opening shot and the shot of the crewmates talking to the captain and then move on to the next'

[05:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scene. Now this next generation is a perfect'

[06:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'example why you should read through your prompts and be careful with the wording because the first shot was supposed to be a shot of the captain\'s hand holding the pen, but we forgot to mention that in the prompt which just created a'

[06:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'floating pen instead of the captain writing with the pen. So small mistakes like this in your prompts might become costly because now that shot is unusable for us and we\'re going to have to regenerate it. The second shot of this sequence I was really satisfied with'

[06:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with the captain standing up and looking kind of terrifying, but the third shot had a problem because the consistency in the environment wasn\'t the same uh compared to the first shot with the crewmates. So from this second generation, the only usable part was the'

[06:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'middle shot of the captain standing up. For the next sequence of the shot list, we had the captain pushing the crewmates apart, opening up a chest, and then grabbing a bottle from the chest and pouring them a glass. Now from this one,'

[06:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the first cut completely failed. He shoved them in a weird way, he opened the chest in the wrong direction, but the second shot was really good of him picking up the bottle close to the camera and then from the third shot, I really like the ending of him pouring'

[07:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the bottle into the glass. And for the final sequence, I kind of wanted the feather to float in the air and land next to the bottle that would then create the hero ending card, but unfortunately, this one was a complete'

[07:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'failure. I couldn\'t use anything from this generation and had to rethink how to end the commercial, but from this first run of generations, we got some really solid shots that are definitely usable in the final commercial. Now we just have to generate the parts in'

[07:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'between these shots to tie everything together. So I went back to Claude and explained which shots I needed to regenerate and then it used the shot list director skill and wrote new sequences for me. So, now we had a good shot of the captain riding but the other'

[07:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shots from that generation was complete trash because I again forgot to mention his hand which created a floating pen in the generation. I then created another version of the pen snapping, him'

[08:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'standing up, and the crewmates looking terrified and from that generation I was only satisfied with the pen snapping part. Now, to solve the positioning of the crewmates within the captain\'s quarters, we were having problems with them being positioned in front of'

[08:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'different backgrounds. I actually took a screenshot of the first generation where I liked their positioning and then used that as a reference image for when the captain walks up to them and they are looking terrified. And as you can see,'

[08:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it helped out massively for this generation because now most of this sequence is totally usable for the final commercial. The only weird part here was the captain shoving the crewmates in a weird way, but I actually made that work'

[08:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in a more comedic way in post which I\'m going to show you in just a little bit. But now I only had two generations left to stay within the 10 generation budget I had set in the beginning and I only'

[08:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'needed to generate the bottle being placed on the table and the first one would have been perfect but the bottle doesn\'t look like the real bottle itself. But the second generation worked out perfectly and I had all the material needed for the commercial and it was'

[09:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'time to jump into post. Now, looking at the timeline for this one, there\'s a few things that you should notice. The first one is of course the cutting of the clips. I have deleted all the bad generations and the bad parts that I'

[09:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'didn\'t like and then reordered everything in the way I wanted to be for the final cut. Then I used the sound effects from the clips themselves because when we generate with this workflow, we always generate with sound'

[09:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'effects but without music. As you can see here on the timeline, I have different sound effects from the clips that extend over each other to tie the clips and cuts together in a seamless way. So, as an example, I have the sound'

[09:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'effect of the captain writing on the paper and also the crewmates talking to the captain begin before they are on the screen. &gt;&gt; Captain, the the rum it\'s gone.'

[09:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; So, using sound effects this way really blends your clips and cuts together in a more seamless way and you avoid this weird cut between clips which might have different sound effects. And for the'

[10:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'music, I chose a track myself and you can see in the timeline I used three different parts, one for the beginning, one for the middle, and also one for the ending. And the final trick I did for this commercial to avoid inconsistencies'

[10:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and small details I didn\'t like is I zoomed in some of the shots to kind of hide something out of screen that I didn\'t want to include in the final edit. For example, here when the captain'

[10:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is writing in the original generation, if you look closely, it doesn\'t really look like he\'s writing anything. So, instead of just regenerating that clip, I zoomed it in a little bit so we couldn\'t see the pen touching the paper'

[10:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and that way the generation was completely usable. So, when you get your results from SeaDance, if there\'s a small detail that you don\'t like, you might be able to crop it out if the rest of the generation is totally usable. I'

[10:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually used this trick in the fragrance and the glow mask commercial as well, which we\'re going to look at now. &gt;&gt; [music]'

[11:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Ah!'

[11:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Now for this one, I used the exact same'

[11:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'process as before creating the character reference sheets for the different outfits, removing the head from the full body reference, then creating the product sheet with the close-up of the label and also the location outside her apartment. And the whole of the concept'

[11:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'was glow after dark as the product was a glow mask, so she would be walking down the street and different elements would be glowing up around her kind of referencing to her glowing skin. And for'

[12:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this one, I want to focus on the consistency because it\'s easier to create a concept where a character is moving through a city like this from location to location instead of staying in one environment where the objects and'

[12:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'positioning has to be consistent like in the pirate example where we first had problems with the crewmates having different backgrounds behind them from generation to generation. And again, I gave myself a 10-generation budget and created the shot list again with the'

[12:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'help of Claude and the first generation turned out quite bad. This kind of commercial really needs more dynamic camera movement to really capture the attention of the viewer at the beginning and also convey the energy of the'

[12:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'commercial. So the only usable part from this first generations was this macro close-up of the cream being applied into her hand. And here I actually decided to regenerate that one and also combine it with the dressing montage of her putting'

[13:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on the clothes. Now this second generation I was really satisfied with because I really like the camera move at the beginning. I like the close-up of her applying the cream to her face and then the clothing montage worked out perfectly. Now this next generation'

[13:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'comes with the biggest inconsistency of the commercial. Let me know if you caught it in the final edit, but she\'s walking down the street past this guy fixing a neon sign. In the first cut, she\'s walking through between the ladder'

[13:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and the building looking up at the guy. But then when we cut to the wide shot where the sign is visible, the guy is super close to the building with his ladder and she has walked past on the other side. I left this error in on'

[13:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'purpose to stay within the budget, but also so that you can think about the details when generating sequences because it might seem like a small thing, but if the character consistency or the positioning of the character in'

[13:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the sequences isn\'t consistent from shot to shot, it feels like something is off about the sequence and it doesn\'t really feel like a cohesive product. So, these are the small details that are important to think about. Another inconsistency'

[14:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'example from the same commercial is the sequence when she walks past the boy in the window. It was actually generated with her walking from left to right on screen past the boy. And if you would cut that together with a shot of her'

[14:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'walking in the opposite direction, it wouldn\'t really feel natural. So, what I did was I took that generation and flipped it in post so she\'s walking in the same direction in both shots. I actually did a similar thing for the'

[14:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ending shot of this commercial. First, I wanted it to be a slow push in towards the product, but it zoomed in too far in the generation result. So, I used that generation and reversed it so it zooms'

[14:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'out at the ending of the commercial. And these are again small tricks that can save you credits because the quality of the generations were good, they just needed a few tweaks to make them usable for the final commercial. And this one'

[14:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'was even more simple than the pirate one in post. I just added the music and the ending text and for the first opening shot, I again zoomed in a little bit to remove the part where she\'s trying to use the product in the wrong way. So,'

[15:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that way we could still use the generation without having to include that small error. Now, let\'s move on to the fragrance commercial. &gt;&gt; [music]'

[15:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; For the fragrance commercial, I wanted'

[15:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to use myself again as the main character. And here I noticed an important thing when it comes to the character reference sheets. The lighting of the characters in the reference sheets heavily impacts how the character will look in the final video generation.'

[15:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You can see in the character reference sheets I have this bright studio lighting on me, which also reflects in the video generation as well. So, when you create your character reference sheets, I would recommend to have more'

[16:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cinematic or flat lighting on the characters because that way it won\'t impact how they look in your final video generation. For example, if we compare the pirates in this shot that were generated with Soul Cinema and then my'

[16:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'character that was generated based off a studio image of me, you can see that the difference in the quality and how it looks on screen is completely different. The pirates look more like they belong in that environment because the'

[16:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reference sheet was closer to the final product, while my character has something slightly off with the lighting on the character compared to the background. able to generate this commercial using seven 15-second long C 2 A S generations. And if you look'

[16:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'through the results from the generations from the link below, you can definitely see that there\'s some weirdness going on in some of the shots. For example, in this one, I was standing inside of the mirror but not outside it but when I'

[17:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'placed the product at the ending of the commercial, but using the best parts from the different generations, we still got somewhat of a cohesive story that worked as a commercial with only seven C 2 A S generations, which I think is'

[17:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'quite impressive. After playing around with this workflow in different product contexts, you can definitely see that you can bring down the cost of the whole project quite massively if you just have a solid and scripted idea. You lock in'

[17:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the elements in the beginning along with the locations, the characters, and the props. And when using the Shot List Director skill, read through the prompts carefully to avoid any type of weirdness'

[17:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'when generating the videos, and use this structure of taking the best parts from the generations and then going back and forth with Claude to then fill out the blanks of the commercial and then editing everything together in post.'

[17:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Hopefully, you learned something from this video, and if you did, consider leaving a like on the video. It\'s a simple and free way for you to support me and the channel. If you want to learn more about AI and product photography,'

[18:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'make sure to check out the School community from the description below. But, other than that, see you in the next one.'
